  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Is Interior Sealing Necessary for Log Homes?",
    "text": "Is Interior Sealing Necessary for Log Homes?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "It's a common question, and a good one: do your interior log home walls really need sealing? The short answer is usually yes, especially after staining. While interior logs aren't exposed to the harsh elements like exterior ones, a clear coat or sealer does a lot to protect the stain and lock in that beautiful appearance. Think of it as putting a protective layer over your artwork. This helps guard against everyday wear and tear, and makes your logs easier to clean. Even if your logs are stained, applying a sealer helps enhance the depth of the color and provides an extra layer of durability. The key steps are to always seal over clean, dry wood – seriously, make sure it’s spotless and completely dry! You'll also need to allow enough drying time between prep, staining, and then applying the topcoat or sealer. We even look for moisture levels below 18% before starting to ensure a perfect bond. If you're dealing with older interiors, or you’re unsure about moisture control or product compatibility, that’s when a professional can step in and ensure everything is done correctly for a lasting, beautiful finish. Don't skip the seal if you want your logs to truly shine!",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
}